 \paragraph{Distance in multidimensional BC models.}
-Euclidean distance is the usual default for vector opinions \citep{fortunato2005,lorenz2007}.
-\citet{lorenz2008} compared the $L_1$ and $L_\infty$ norms after rescaling the threshold so that their confidence regions had equal volume; \citet{huet2008} let agents reject one another on a single dimension; \citet{kurahashi2016} studied how robust clustering is across generalised BC models; angular measures \citep{schweighofer2020}, topic spaces with correlated dimensions \citep{baumann2021} and topic-weighted discordances \citep{li2026} have followed.
+Most multidimensional BC models use Euclidean distance \citep{lorenz2007}, and comparing confidence regions of different shapes is almost as old: \citet{fortunato2005} tried several shapes for two-dimensional opinions in the Hegselmann--Krause model and found consensus thresholds close to the one-dimensional ones.
+\citet{lorenz2008} compared the $L_1$ and $L_\infty$ norms after rescaling the threshold so that their confidence regions had equal volume; \citet{huet2008} added rejection when two agents are close on one dimension and far on the other, which changes how the number of clusters grows as the threshold shrinks; angular measures \citep{schweighofer2020}, topic spaces whose topics overlap \citep{baumann2021} and topic-weighted discordances \citep{li2026} have followed.
+These studies show that the shape of the confidence region matters; our concern is that a comparison of shapes is easily confounded with a comparison of sizes.
+\citet{kurahashi2016} showed that adding rare random influences makes clustering robust under relaxed bounded confidence, a reminder that small changes to the dynamics can move the outcomes we compare.
 \citet{li2026} note that lowering their topic weight at a fixed bound enlarges the region of agreement as well as changing its shape, which is the confound behind our first choice.
 \citet{flache2017} review social-influence models more widely and call for this kind of systematic comparison.
 
